mes something like  beneficence so that when you need them  to help you they'll be there he also  goes on and he says there really is no  motive for transgressing the desires  that spring from nature are easily  gratified without doing any man wrong so  we're again we're talking about rational  sane people who restrict their desires  and pleasures to those that are easily  satisfied those that are natural to us  those of you that are imaginary ought to  be resisted for they set their  affections on nothing that is really  wanted and then he says there's more  loss and Herenton and justice itself  than there is profit in the gain that it  brings so if you are unjust you're  losing something for yourself even if  you're getting something so the the rich  the powerful the you know smart the  talented they need to be just just as  much as what Cicero probably would call  the little people do as well so again  this is the epicurean position this is  not you know all virtue ethics and this  is actually not Cicero's own point of  view this is that of the character  torquatus who is presenting it the  viewpoint is that justice is something  that we ought to have we ought to  cultivate we ought to view as a virtue  not because it's good in itself but  because in the way that it works when we  break it down it leads to a better life  for the person who is just
 

